{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778941297,"nonce":"8BgTu+gBGPji7ZVpC+XxyjKEZLc=","message":"## Revised implementation addendum: gates, schema corrections, and child-issue boundaries\n\nThis addendum supersedes the unsafe parts of comment 049671d while preserving the core direction: Mandible owns the local hot path, git-bug becomes an adapter, and graph/UI projections read from typed ledger state.\n\n### Critical corrections before implementation\n\n1. Treat `ledger_events` plus an adapter outbox as the sync source, not a single `sync_state` flag on issues. Comments, labels, status changes, relationships, auth links, and work-unit transitions all need export/import visibility without diff guessing.\n2. Store adapter cursors/version state for external records. For git-bug, keep the last seen/imported/exported remote version or content hash per external issue/comment/label where available, so Mandible can detect real external divergence.\n3. Make conflicts first-class records with explicit UX actions: keep Mandible, accept external, or manually merge. Warnings alone are not sufficient.\n4. Add a logical project/repo identity layer. Existing `work_repos.root` is checkout-path identity and is not safe as the canonical issue namespace. Multiple local clones/worktrees of the same remote should map to one logical project unless explicitly configured otherwise.\n5. Avoid nullable uniqueness traps. Alias rows should use explicit non-null namespace fields, e.g. `project_id TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''`, rather than relying on nullable composite keys.\n6. Use strict canonical link tables for core relationships. A general polymorphic edge table is acceptable as a graph projection, but canonical issue/auth/session/work-unit links need enforceable tables and/or triggers.\n7. Enable and test SQLite referential integrity for the ledger path. If some legacy tables cannot use FKs yet, ledger tables should still use FKs where practical and tests should catch dangling canonical links.\n8. Update backup/export coverage. The existing Mandible backup path must include ledger current-state tables, event/outbox tables, adapter cursors, conflict records, and canonical link tables.\n9. Include project/global instruction reflection. This changes issue tracking, commit trailers, auth resolution, and fallback git-bug behavior, so a paired dotfiles/global-conventions issue is required.\n10. Add performance gates. The ledger is partly motivated by git-bug lock and query slowness, so import, issue-list, issue-detail, graph, and sync operations need explicit benchmark targets and regression tests where feasible.\n\n### Canonical IDs\n\nUse Mandible-owned globally unique IDs for canonical records, not git-bug IDs or local paths. Recommended first implementation:\n\n- issue IDs: `iss_\u003cuuidv7-or-equivalent\u003e`;\n- comment IDs: `com_\u003cuuidv7-or-equivalent\u003e`;\n- event IDs: `evt_\u003cuuidv7-or-equivalent\u003e`;\n- relationship/link IDs: `rel_\u003cuuidv7-or-equivalent\u003e` where a synthetic ID is needed;\n- external aliases preserve git-bug IDs, short prefixes, future GitHub issue IDs, and any user-facing shorthand.\n\nA UUIDv7-equivalent helper is preferable because chronological ordering helps debugging and event replay. If implementation cost is not worth it immediately, a clearly named local monotonic timestamp-plus-random helper is acceptable; do not derive canonical IDs from repo path, branch, or git-bug short ID.\n\n### Enumerations and state machines\n\nIssue status and work-unit state should remain separate state machines:\n\n- Issue status: `open`, `closed`.\n- Issue resolution: `fixed`, `merged`, `duplicate`, `wontfix`, `invalid`, `obsolete`, `unknown`, nullable while open.\n- Issue kind: `bug`, `feature`, `umbrella`, `task`, `question`, `maintenance`, `deferred`, `unknown`.\n- Priority: `low`, `normal`, `high`, `urgent`, nullable/unknown for imported records.\n- Actor type: `mandible_user`, `agent`, `system`, `external_adapter`.\n- Event source: `mandible`, `git_bug`, `auth_queue`, `session`, `work_unit`, `import`, `system`.\n- Outbox status: `pending`, `processing`, `applied`, `failed`, `conflicted`, `skipped`.\n- Sync job status: `queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled`.\n- Relationship types, first cut: `parent_child`, `blocks`, `blocked_by`, `duplicate_of`, `related`, `references`, `fixed_by`, `caused_by`, `implements`, `tests`, `auth_resolves`, `session_worked_on`, `work_unit_tracks`.\n- Relationship provenance/confidence: `observed`, `derived`, `imported`, `manual`, `low`, `medium`, `high` as separate fields where needed. Avoid vague `relationship_class` unless its legal values and purpose are specified.\n\n### Migration gate sequence\n\nThe cutover must not be a flip-the-switch migration.\n\n1. Build ledger schema/service/events/outbox/project identity behind existing behavior.\n2. Import git-bug into ledger idempotently and compare ledger projections against current git-bug scanner output on real repos.\n3. Add export/reconciliation/conflict records before any write cutover.\n4. Run shadow reads and reconciliation reports: issue count, status, labels, comments, parent/child links, aliases, auth links, work-unit links.\n5. Dual-write or command-gate selected mutations where safe, while retaining rollback to git-bug scanner reads.\n6. Switch issue routes/CLI reads only after reconciliation is clean enough and failures are visible.\n7. Switch writes only when export/outbox/conflict UX exists; do not create a deployment window where ledger writes cannot be mirrored to git-bug.\n8. Keep compatibility fields and git-bug scanner fallback until the corpus has passed migration verification.\n\n### Graph/UI sequencing\n\nThe graph and issue UI should be designed against ledger contracts now, but they should not be bundled into the same irreversible cutover as ledger core.\n\n- Ledger core defines graph projection records and relationship semantics.\n- Graph migration consumes those records behind feature flags or mode-specific gates.\n- Issue detail/comment UI should share the ledger detail API, but old issue reads can remain available during shadow mode.\n- Auth requests are issue-attached evidence in issue mode and standalone nodes in auth/work modes.\n- Dead sessions belong in issue history by default; active/idle sessions can render as live actor glyphs/overlays once reliable session links exist.\n\n### Child-issue split\n\nThis umbrella should be executed through gated child issues, each with its own branch and verification:\n\n1. Ledger core schema, ID/project identity, service boundary, events, outbox, backups, and tests.\n2. Git-bug import/export adapter, namespaced labels, remote cursors, idempotency, and conflict records.\n3. Migration/shadow/dual-write gates plus issue route and CLI cutover planning.\n4. Work-unit/auth/session/commit integration on canonical ledger IDs.\n5. Graph projection migration from git-bug/body parsing to ledger relationships and live overlays.\n6. Issue detail UI, comments, conflict/sync UX, and shared graph detail component.\n7. Dotfiles/global convention updates for ledger-era issue tracking and commit trailers.\n\n### Gate discipline\n\nEach child issue should state: what it changes, what remains compatibility-only, rollback path, data-loss risk, tests, and how it proves it did not regress the current git-bug workflow. Integration into protected `main` should happen through gated branches rather than one monolithic branch.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}